# Anemos Playground

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:anemos, path: Path.expand("../", __DIR__)}
])
```

## The Pipeline

Anemos processes `.rule` files through a pipeline: **Lexer → Parser → Compiler → Dispatcher**.

Let's walk through each stage.

## 1. Lexing

The lexer turns source text into tokens.

```elixir
source = ~S|rule lights { when MOTION { [DEVICE::set "light" "on"] } }|

{:ok, tokens} = Anemos.Lexer.tokenize(source)
```

Try changing the source and re-running. Notice how keywords, module IDs,
strings, and operators each get their own token type.

## 2. Parsing

The parser turns tokens into a typed AST.

```elixir
{:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(source)
```

Inspect the structure — every node carries a `:line` for error reporting:

```elixir
[rule] = ast.declarations
IO.inspect(rule.name, label: "rule name")
IO.inspect(rule.body, label: "body")
```

## 3. Compiling

The compiler produces executable closures and process specs.

```elixir
{:ok, compiled} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)

IO.inspect(length(compiled.rules), label: "rules")
IO.inspect(length(compiled.conditions), label: "conditions")
IO.inspect(length(compiled.capabilities), label: "capabilities")
```

Each compiled rule has a handler function you can call directly:

```elixir
[rule] = compiled.rules
IO.inspect(rule.subscriptions, label: "listens for")

{:ok, ops} = rule.handler.("MOTION", %{})
IO.inspect(ops, label: "operations")
```

## 4. Full Pipeline

`Anemos.run/2` does all of the above in one call:

```elixir
{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(source, event: "MOTION")
IO.inspect(results)
```

## Garage Lights

The classic example — motion sensor controls lights.

```elixir
garage = """
rule garage-lights-on {
  context "garage.motion.sensor" {
    when SENSOR_MOTION_DETECTED {
      [DEVICE::set "garage.lights" "on"]
    }
  }
}

rule garage-lights-off {
  context "garage.motion.sensor" {
    when SENSOR_NO_MOTION for 10min {
      [DEVICE::set "garage.lights" "off"]
    }
  }
}
"""

{:ok, on_results} = Anemos.run(garage, event: "SENSOR_MOTION_DETECTED")
IO.inspect(on_results, label: "motion detected")

{:ok, off_results} = Anemos.run(garage, event: "SENSOR_NO_MOTION")
IO.inspect(off_results, label: "no motion")
```

## Conditions

Conditions are stateful — they track boolean state and emit synthetic events
when transitioning from false to true.

```elixir
policy = """
condition temp_high {
  when TEMP_READING_HIGH {
    log "temperature threshold exceeded"
  }
}

rule alert-on-temp {
  when CONDITION_temp_high {
    log "ALERT: temperature is high"
    [DEVICE::set "fan" "on"]
  }
}
"""

{:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(policy)
{:ok, compiled} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)

IO.inspect(hd(compiled.conditions).name, label: "condition")
IO.inspect(hd(compiled.conditions).subscriptions, label: "watches for")
IO.inspect(hd(compiled.rules).subscriptions, label: "rule listens for")
```

Now run it — the raw event triggers the condition, which fires the rule:

```elixir
{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(policy, event: "TEMP_READING_HIGH")
IO.inspect(results, label: "results")
```

## Capabilities and Demand

Capabilities are passive actions invoked via `demand`. They use PAM-style
control flags for failure semantics.

```elixir
policy = """
capability audit-key {
  context security

  on demand {
    log "audit key unlocked"
  }
}

rule secure-action {
  when SECURE_EVENT {
    demand audit-key
    log "proceeding with secure action"
  }
}
"""

{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(policy, event: "SECURE_EVENT")
IO.inspect(results, label: "results")
```

Try adding `--optional` to the demand and changing the capability name
to something that doesn't exist — see how PAM flags affect behavior.

## Variable Sigils

Three sigils with distinct resolution paths:

* `?name` — resolve from external context data
* `@name` — immutable binding (created by `let`)
* `$name` — mutable binding (created by `set`)

```elixir
policy = """
rule greet {
  when GREET_EVENT {
    let greeting = "hello"
    set count = 1
    log @greeting
    log ?user
  }
}
"""

# Pass context data for ?user resolution
context = %{context_data: %{"user" => "ian"}}
{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(policy, event: "GREET_EVENT", context: context)
IO.inspect(results, label: "results")
```

## Demand Flags

PAM-style control flags govern failure behavior in demand stacks.

```elixir
policy = """
capability required-cap {
  context test

  on demand {
    log "required capability met"
  }
}

rule stacked-demands {
  when TEST_EVENT {
    demand required-cap
    demand nonexistent-cap --optional
    log "both demands evaluated"
  }
}
"""

{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(policy, event: "TEST_EVENT")
IO.inspect(results, label: "with --optional on missing cap")
```

The `--optional` flag lets the rule continue even though `nonexistent-cap`
doesn't exist. Try removing `--optional` and see what happens.

## The Full ddns.rule

The most complex example — condition, capability, and rule working together.

```elixir
source = File.read!(Path.expand("../priv/examples/ddns.rule", __DIR__))
IO.puts(source)
```

```elixir
{:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(source)
{:ok, compiled} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)

IO.puts("Declarations compiled:")
IO.puts("  #{length(compiled.rules)} rule(s)")
IO.puts("  #{length(compiled.conditions)} condition(s)")
IO.puts("  #{length(compiled.capabilities)} capability(s)")
IO.puts("")

for cond <- compiled.conditions do
  IO.puts("Condition '#{cond.name}' watches: #{inspect(cond.subscriptions)}")
end

for rule <- compiled.rules do
  IO.puts("Rule '#{rule.name}' listens for: #{inspect(rule.subscriptions)}")
end

for cap <- compiled.capabilities do
  IO.puts("Capability '#{cap.name}' in context: #{cap.context}")
end
```

## Supervised Runtime

The normal way to use Anemos is as a supervised interpreter. Start it blank,
then load modules and policies dynamically.

```elixir
# Start a supervised runtime
{:ok, _sup} = Supervisor.start_link(
  [{Anemos.Runtime, name: :playground}],
  strategy: :one_for_one
)

# Register a module
Anemos.Runtime.register(:playground, "DEVICE", Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device)

# Load a policy
Anemos.Runtime.load(:playground, """
rule lights-on {
  when MOTION {
    [DEVICE::set "light" "on"]
  }
}
""")

# Dispatch an event
{:ok, results} = Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:playground, "MOTION")
IO.inspect(results, label: "dispatch results")
```

Load more policies into the same runtime — they accumulate:

```elixir
Anemos.Runtime.load(:playground, """
condition alarm {
  when INTRUSION_DETECTED {
    log "alarm triggered"
  }
}

rule alert {
  when CONDITION_alarm {
    log "ALERT: intrusion!"
    [DEVICE::set "siren" "on"]
  }
}
""")

{:ok, results} = Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:playground, "INTRUSION_DETECTED")
IO.inspect(results, label: "alarm results")

# Query condition state
IO.inspect(Anemos.Runtime.condition_state(:playground, "alarm"), label: "alarm active?")
```

## Scheduled Triggers

`at` blocks are clock-driven event sources — they fire on a schedule
without needing an external event.

```elixir
{:ok, _sup} = Supervisor.start_link(
  [{Anemos.Runtime, name: :scheduled}],
  strategy: :one_for_one
)

Anemos.Runtime.load(:scheduled, """
rule heartbeat {
  at interval(100ms) {
    log "tick"
  }
}
""")

# Wait and see ticks in the log output
Process.sleep(350)
IO.puts("(check log output above for tick messages)")
```

`at` also works with specific times and recurrence:

```elixir
# These compile and schedule — they won't fire in the playground
# unless you happen to be running at the right time, but they
# demonstrate the syntax.

{:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse("""
condition statuscheck {
  at 11:00 every weekday {
    log "time for standup"
  }
}

rule deadline {
  at DATE_2026.12.31 {
    log "happy new year"
  }
}

rule tomorrow-task {
  at NLD_tomorrow {
    log "reminder: do the thing"
  }
}

rule weekly-review {
  at NLD_next.friday {
    log "time for weekly review"
  }
}

rule end-of-month {
  at NLD_end.of.month {
    log "reports due"
  }
}
""")

{:ok, compiled} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)

IO.puts("Schedules compiled:")
for sched <- compiled.schedules do
  IO.puts("  #{sched.event_name} — #{inspect(sched.time_spec)}, recurrence: #{inspect(sched.recurrence)}")
end
```

Natural language date expressions resolve at schedule time:

```elixir
# See what NLD expressions resolve to
for expr <- ~w(today tomorrow next.wednesday next.week end.of.month next.year) do
  case Anemos.Runtime.NLD.resolve(expr) do
    {:ok, dt} -> IO.puts("  NLD_#{expr} → #{NaiveDateTime.to_string(dt)}")
    {:error, _} -> IO.puts("  NLD_#{expr} → (unknown)")
  end
end
```

## Loops and Flow Control

Iterate over collections, repeat while conditions hold, and control execution flow.

```elixir
# for-in loop with early exit via return
loop_source = """
rule scan {
  when SCAN {
    for item in ?items {
      if @item == "stop" {
        return
      }
      log @item
    }
  }
}
"""

context = %{context_data: %{"items" => ["alpha", "bravo", "stop", "charlie"]}}
{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(loop_source, event: "SCAN", context: context)
IO.puts("for-in with early return:")
for %{type: :log, value: v} <- results, do: IO.puts("  #{v}")
```

```elixir
# while loop accumulating state
while_source = """
rule accumulate {
  when TICK {
    set total = 0
    set i = 1
    while $i <= 5 {
      set total = $total + $i
      set i = $i + 1
    }
    log $total
  }
}
"""

{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(while_source, event: "TICK")
IO.puts("while loop sum 1..5:")
for %{type: :log, value: v} <- results, do: IO.puts("  #{v}")
```

```elixir
# nextevent chains within the same rule
chain_source = """
rule pipeline {
  when START {
    log "phase-1"
    nextevent PHASE_TWO
  }

  when PHASE_TWO {
    log "phase-2"
  }
}
"""

{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(chain_source, event: "START")
IO.puts("nextevent chain:")
for %{type: :log, value: v} <- results, do: IO.puts("  #{v}")
```

## Sandbox

Write your own policy below and experiment.

```elixir
my_policy = """
; Write your policy here

condition statuscheck {
  at (11:00 and 16:00) every workday 
}

rule watch-my-active-projects {
  context mission.dev.projects

  when CONDITION_statuscheck {
    filter :p001 {:worksOn ?project} 
      |> map ?project {:status :active} 
      |> reduce counter ?project
      |> emit [EVENT::publish counter to channel(active.projects.count)]
  }
}
"""

{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(my_policy, event: "MY_EVENT")
IO.inspect(results)
```
